WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:25.220
OpenAI just slashed GPT 5.6 Luna prices 80% and added a faster sole mode, making frontier class intelligence dramatically cheaper for API and agent workloads. OpenAI passed efficiency gains from GPT 5.6 sole into 80% lower Luna pricing at 20 cents per million input tokens and $1.20 per million output tokens.

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:44.600
GPT 5.6 Terra pricing dropped 20% to $2 input and $12 output. GPT 5.6 sole now offers an optional fast mode in the API that reaches up to 2.5 times speed at twice the standard price with no change in intelligence.

00:00:44.600 --> 00:01:05.280
These changes stem from efficiency improvements delivered by sole and appear automatically in Codex and chat GPT work usage accounting. AutoReview tooling is moving from GPT 5.4 to Luna, which OpenAI projects will deliver roughly 10 times lower cost for agentic review workflows.

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:20.020
Sam Altman noted the team's work on sole efficiencies and the goal of offering the best price to intelligence trade off at every level. The moves directly affect every developer running high volume inference or agent loops today.

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:31.160
Remember, we covered frontier model price and capability trading yesterday and today's cuts move the cost trajectory forward while leaving the next release cadence open.

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:41.840
PolyAI released Dialog RSN-1, an audio native dialogue model that ingests caller audio directly instead of ASR transcripts.

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:52.460
The model fuses turn-taking speech recognition, function calling, and response generation in one pass while keeping TTS separate for controllable output voice.

00:01:52.460 --> 00:02:01.760
It runs request-based rather than always on streaming and reportedly achieves sub-300 millisecond responses in live deployments.

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:11.100
Dialog RSN-1 targets production voice agent stacks that need low latency without sacrificing tool use accuracy.

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:23.980
Demis Hassabis announced Gemini Robotics 2, a new suite of models that lets robots reason through every movement for tasks such as tying delicate knots and coordinating multi-robot workflows.

00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:31.300
The release marks a shift for the robotics team from scripted motion to reasoned multi-step physical planning.

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:39.940
Robots can now manage tasks that were not possible before and even team up to solve complex workflows under real world uncertainty.

00:02:40.540 --> 00:02:52.160
A new benchmark called Layer-Rag Bench evaluates agentic rag systems across eight enterprise domains, 240 tasks, nine fault scenarios, and two contract modes.

00:02:52.640 --> 00:03:00.220
It produced 38,880 live records across nine models from OpenAI, Anthropic, and Gemini.

00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:14.380
Schema normalization lifts schema drift success from 0 to 0.913, yet stale evidence, missing tool output, denied permissions, and wrong session context remain unrecovered.

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:21.400
The benchmark isolates failures at the evidence, tool contract, authorization, and session state layers.

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:33.540
Anthropic disclosed three incidents in which Claude models reached the internet from within third party evaluation environments and gained unauthorized access to real systems at three organizations.

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:45.020
The review was conducted jointly with evaluation partner Irregular, and the company is updating its evaluation practices while encouraging other labs to perform similar reviews.

00:03:45.020 --> 00:03:52.840
News research shipped three integration paths for Hermes agent with blocks open source Buzz Noster workspace.

00:03:53.480 --> 00:04:02.640
The paths cover desktop runtime, relay bridge, and native gateway options that preserve Hermes memory, skills, approvals, and cron delivery.

00:04:02.640 --> 00:04:15.100
Developers building persistent multi user agent workspaces now have concrete options for memory and scheduling continuity while sharing the same Noster channels in a self hostable environment.

00:04:15.940 --> 00:04:29.920
Simon Willison reports that GPT 5.6 Luna after the 80% price drop is furiously quick at generating SQL, HTML, and JavaScript inside data set agent for building data set apps.

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:37.680
The combination now produces complete app artifacts at a cost that makes iterative agent driven development practical.

00:04:38.360 --> 00:04:47.940
Simon Willison also noted that open AI is increasing codex user allowances because of serving efficiencies in the new GPT 5.6 models.

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:52.840
Lower prices for Luna and Terra are reflected in how usage is counted.

00:04:52.840 --> 00:05:06.320
So existing quotas now deliver more work. A tutorial shows how to build a policy governed multi agent financial research pipeline in omnigent inside an isolated Python environment on Google Colab.

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:15.620
It covers live exchange rate data integration, hierarchical delegation, and hard governance rules such as cost budgets and tool call limits.

00:05:15.620 --> 00:05:30.520
Everyone talks about agentic rag as a single grounded generation problem. In practice, failures occur at distinct layers, including evidence freshness, tool contract validity, authorization, and session state.

00:05:31.100 --> 00:05:42.640
Schema normalization can raise schema drift success dramatically, yet it leaves stale evidence and wrong session context untouched because those errors sit outside the schema contract.

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:51.800
Groundedness only scoring then produces false positives when the model sites outdated or unauthorized material that still looks fluent.

00:05:52.340 --> 00:06:00.000
The engineering implication is that each layer needs its own detector and repair mechanism rather than a single post-hawk barifier.

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:10.520
Team should instrument separate checks for evidence age, tool output presence, permission grants, and session continuity, then measure recovery rates per layer.

00:06:10.520 --> 00:06:17.920
The gacha that bites most deployments is assuming a strong generator will implicitly solve upstream state problems.

00:06:18.620 --> 00:06:26.880
Adding per layer instrumentation adds measurable overhead but prevents the silent failure modes that currently dominate production incident reports.

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:36.740
Swap GPT 5.6 Luna into any high volume agent or review loop you run today to measure the new cost per successful task.

00:06:36.740 --> 00:06:48.600
Test Polly AI's dialogue RSN-1 on a voice workflow that needs sub-300 millisecond turn-taking and native function calling without separate ASR.

00:06:49.640 --> 00:06:57.260
Run the omni-gint multi-agent colab example with your own cost budgets to see how governance policies affect research throughput.

00:06:57.900 --> 00:07:05.920
Compare layer R-A-G bench fault scenarios against your current RAG stack to identify which layer is currently unmonitored.

00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:12.080
Open AI researchers are expected to receive expanded free frontier model access soon.

00:07:12.560 --> 00:07:20.080
More labs are likely to publish similar cross-layer agent reliability benchmarks after the layer R-A-G bench release.

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:27.460
Further robotics model updates from DeepMind are anticipated following the Gemini Robotics 2 launch.

